#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""warmup -- the warm card, the check, and the delta (proposal 19, W-05, D1).

  warmup [--project DIR]                     print the warm card
  warmup --check                             exit 1 if anything verifiable is missing or stale
  warmup --json                              the card's state as JSON
  warmup --state PATH                        also save the state (for a later --since)
  warmup --since PATH                        print only what moved since that state
  warmup --reheat                            delta since the last warmup/reheat, plus the standard (proposal 28, R-01)
  warmup --queue                             with the plain card or --reheat: record what is pending as ledger items
  warmup --context TEXT                      one free-text line, carried into the state file
  warmup --no-pull                           never fast-forward the shared rules checkout (proposal 28, R-05)
  warmup --no-recall | --recall-corpus GLOB  control the recall step

RUNNING VS FRESH (proposal 28, R-01). A fresh lead runs the plain card;
a lead already running asks only what moved -- `--reheat` is `--since` against
a state file warmup itself keeps (default: inside the project's own .git/, so
nothing here is committed), with the standard's own status appended every
time rather than only when it changed, since a running lead needs to be told
what it did not just ask about. Both the plain card and --reheat run
conformance (bin/conformance's twelve items) and rulecheck's mandatory-pending
check, and show every item that does not hold. `--queue` writes: each
non-holding conformance item and each pending mandatory Standard change
becomes a ledger item (tools/tracker/queue.py), owner lead, status not
started, placed first in that ledger's own item list -- idempotent, so running
it again adds nothing for what is already queued. Without --queue only the
card lines are printed; nothing is written.

A cold session and a running one ask the same question -- what is the state,
and what do I read -- and before this they were answered by the sponsor
typing it: "reread the handoff", "are you using ruflo", "update the plan and
show the burn down" (six times in one engine session), "what is the current
status" (five). The card answers it from disk, in the same order every time,
and asks nothing.

WHAT --check FAILS ON, and what it only shows. It fails on what can be
verified: a missing standard file, an invalid ledger, the project's one
tracker page (docs/proposals/tracker/index.html, `tracker board`, proposal 22
T-02) missing or no longer matching the ledgers, a per-ledger page only for a
ledger that records its own tracker (`tracker.own`), a checkpoint whose digest
no longer matches its ledger, a file in the declared
read order (HANDOFF.md and docs/OPERATING-RULES.md, by default) still
carrying {{PLACEHOLDERS}} a seed left for the lead, and a declared
safety_rules heading that is not in its file (proposal 20, V-02), and each
CHANGELOG entry since the project's .common-rules-version stamp that carries a
`**Standard change (mandatory):**` line (proposal 21, S-09) -- the card lists
them under `rules  behind · N mandatory Standard change(s) to implement
first:`, from rulecheck's own mandatory_pending(). A stamp behind only on
informational entries is STALE, shown and never failed; no stamp is "not
adopted" with a count of the CHANGELOG's mandatory entries, never failed. It shows,
and never fails on, how many commits the prose files are behind HEAD: prose
cannot be digest-checked, and failing on a count of commits punishes the
busiest projects rather than the neglected ones.

A PROJECT'S OWN ENTRY POINTS (proposal 20, D9). A project that declares them
in .common-rules.json (see tools/project.py) gets them: the prose checks run
on its read_order instead of HANDOFF.md and OPERATING-RULES.md, prohibitions
come from its safety_rules section, and the card shows its quick and merge
gates. A broken declaration -- malformed, a wrong type, a command that is not
one line, a path outside the project, a declared file that does not exist --
is a problem, and no declared path outside the project is read or hashed. With no
declaration the card and --check are unchanged. --json and --state carry four
more keys either way -- prose, prohibitions_from, quick_gate and
merge_gate_declared -- and tests/test_project_declaration.py pins the set.

PAGE CHANGED SINCE LAST PUBLISH (proposal 20, V-09, D1; proposal 22, T-03). A
page is published only through a session's Artifact tool, which no script can
call; `tracker published` records the URL and the page's digest in a sidecar
beside the page. When the page on disk no longer matches that digest the card
prints `page changed since last publish: <the page> → <url>` -- and --since
prints it when it is news. It is a to-do, not a broken standard, so --check
never fails on it. No sidecar, or switches.publish off: no line. A sidecar
that cannot be read is a problem.

T-03: the page of record is the project's one tracker page,
docs/proposals/tracker/index.html, recorded in index.published.json by
`tracker published --project`; the line names that path, and the record is
read only while switches.publish is on in every ledger. --json/--state carry
it as the top-level `published` key, present only when there is such a
sidecar. A per-ledger sidecar is read only for a ledger that records its own
tracker (`tracker.own`) and for a declared plan_page's record -- one the
project declares, or one the sidecar itself names a `page` in (records_a_page,
so a real publish never goes silent because the declaration moved). A leftover
per-ledger sidecar of any other ledger is never read, prints nothing and is no
problem here -- bin/conformance item 10 is where it is named. Each ledger in
--json/--state still carries `published` (None when nothing is read).

A DECLARED PLAN PAGE (proposal 20, V-11, D1 with D9). A record written with
`tracker published --page <path>` names the file the project's own plan_page
generator writes. The card compares that record's `ledger_digest` with the
ledger on disk, not the page file -- the PhotoVault app's generator stamps
today's date into its page -- and prints `page changed since last publish:
<page> → <url>`. A recorded page that no longer exists, resolves outside
the project, is a symlink or is not a regular file is a problem, and the
chain names which; a malformed `page`, `ledger_digest` or `page_unchanged` is
too. A sidecar that resolves outside the project (a symlinked sidecar or
tracker directory) is a problem and is never read (round 2). `published` then
also carries `page`, `ledger_digest`, `current_ledger_digest` and, when
recorded, `page_unchanged`. A record without `page` behaves as above.

It never runs Ruflo. The CLI auto-starts a daemon that spends tokens until
stopped (measured in the PhotoVault engine, 7 September). Warm-up says where
the CLI is; the loop runs per item, by the lead, who stops what it started.

Exit codes: 0 ready (or a card printed), 1 --check found problems, 2 could
not check (not a git repository).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import json
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

RULES_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(RULES_DIR))

from tools import project as P  # noqa: E402
from tools import ruflo as RU  # noqa: E402
from tools import rules_pull as RP  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import board as B  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import checkpoint as CP  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import ledger as L  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import parts as PT  # noqa: E402, proposal 30, P-04
from tools.tracker import render as R  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import rescore as RS  # noqa: E402

PROSE = tuple(P.DEFAULTS["read_order"])
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r"\{\{[A-Z0-9_]+\}\}")
STOP = {"with", "from", "that", "this", "into", "when", "what", "their", "does", "and", "the", "for"}


def git(project: Path, *args: str) -> str | None:
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(project), *args], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None


def sha256(path: Path) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()


def behind(project: Path, rel: str) -> int | None:
    last = git(project, "log", "-1", "--format=%H", "--", rel)
    if not last:
        return None
    n = git(project, "rev-list", "--count", f"{last}..HEAD")
    return int(n) if n and n.isdigit() else None


def test_command(project: Path) -> str:
    """land's test_cmd(), in the same order with the same tests: gates.merge in
    .common-rules.json, then .common-rules-test, then the guess -- and a
    declaration that cannot be read is land's refusal. Pinned equal to land's
    own answer by tests/test_warmup.py."""
    return P.test_command(project)


def ruflo_cli() -> str | None:
    """The Ruflo binary warm-up would use, or None -- tools/ruflo.py's own
    resolve_binary() (RF-01), shared with bin/ruflo-item and bin/conformance,
    so the three tools can never disagree about which binary is "the" one.
    Only the path is wanted here (never npx's own extra words, which only
    $RUFLO can carry): a multi-word resolution's first word is warm-up's
    answer."""
    argv, _err = RU.resolve_binary()
    return argv[0] if argv else None


def ruflo_namespace(project: Path) -> str:
    """The Ruflo memory namespace this project resolves to -- tools/ruflo.py's
    namespace_for(), shared with bin/ruflo-item and bin/conformance item 9."""
    return RU.namespace_for(project)


def _load_rulecheck():
    """bin/rulecheck as a module: mandatory_pending() is one answer, shared
    with `rulecheck --mandatory` and bin/conformance, never re-derived here."""
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("rulecheck", str(RULES_DIR / "bin" / "rulecheck"))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


def _load_conformance():
    """bin/conformance as a module: the same twelve-item measure() `warmup
    --queue` reads from, and `standard: N of 12 hold` on the card is its own
    count, never re-derived here (proposal 28, R-01)."""
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("conformance", str(RULES_DIR / "bin" / "conformance"))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


RC = _load_rulecheck()
CF = _load_conformance()


def rules_status(project: Path) -> tuple[str, list[str]]:
    """The card's rules value, and the problems it carries (proposal 21, S-09).

    A CHANGELOG entry since the stamp that carries `**Standard change
    (mandatory):**` is work to do before the stamp moves (the sponsor, A-03),
    so each is a problem and --check fails naming it. The value is then
    several lines -- the count, then one shown() heading per line -- joined
    with "\\n". shown() leaves no real newline inside a heading, so the split in
    card() is exact; the state keeps its key set (tests/test_project_declaration.py
    pins it). Behind only on informational entries stays STALE, shown and never
    failed. No stamp stays "not adopted", with a count of the mandatory entries
    in the whole CHANGELOG, and never fails.

    Round 2: a stamp that cannot be resolved (not a sha, or a commit the rules
    repo does not have) fails --check -- it used to read "could not check" and
    pass -- and the card lists every mandatory entry in the CHANGELOG. A stamp
    ahead of the rules checkout is named, and never fails."""
    if not (project / ".common-rules-version").is_file():
        whole = RC.mandatory_in_changelog()
        count = "" if whole is None else f" · {len(whole)} mandatory Standard change(s) in the rules CHANGELOG"
        return "not adopted (.common-rules-version absent)" + count, []
    p = RC.mandatory_pending(project)
    if p.state == "aligned":
        return "aligned", []
    if p.state == "ahead":
        return "ahead of this rules checkout -- update common-rules", []
    if p.state == "unresolvable":
        stamp = "(unreadable)" if p.stamp is None else shown(p.stamp)
        problem = ["rules stamp cannot be resolved -- rulecheck --align after implementing every "
                   "mandatory Standard change"]
        # S-09 final review: --check named no entry; the card alone did.
        problem += [f"mandatory Standard change not implemented: {e.heading} -- implement it first, then "
                    f"rulecheck --align (rulecheck --project {project} --mandatory quotes what it asks)"
                    for e in p.entries]
        if not p.entries:
            return f"stamp {stamp} cannot be resolved · the rules CHANGELOG carries no mandatory Standard change", problem
        lines = [f"stamp {stamp} cannot be resolved · {len(p.entries)} mandatory Standard change(s) "
                 f"to implement first:"]
        return "\n".join(lines + [shown(e.heading) for e in p.entries]), problem
    if p.state == "behind" and p.entries:
        lines = [f"behind · {len(p.entries)} mandatory Standard change(s) to implement first:"]
        lines += [shown(e.heading) for e in p.entries]
        problems = [f"mandatory Standard change not implemented: {e.heading} -- implement it first, then "
                    f"rulecheck --align (rulecheck --project {project} --mandatory quotes what it asks)"
                    for e in p.entries]
        return "\n".join(lines), problems
    if p.state == "behind":
        return "STALE -- run rulecheck and read what changed", []
    return "could not check", []


def prohibitions(project: Path, safety_rules=P.DEFAULTS["safety_rules"], confine: bool = False) -> list[str]:
    """List items and bold-numbered lines, verbatim, from each declared
    FILE#heading: the `## ` section whose heading contains that text. A
    declared source (confine) is read only when it resolves inside the project."""
    out = []
    for rel, heading in P.safety_sources(safety_rules):
        path = project / rel
        if not path.is_file() or (confine and not P.inside(project, rel)):
            continue
        needle, inside = heading.lower(), False
        for line in path.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines():
            if line.startswith("## "):
                # "## Prohibitions, verbatim" in the template; "## Two prohibitions.
                # Verbatim, non-negotiable." in the PhotoVault engine, whose card
                # said "none found" until this matched the word, not the prefix.
                # "## Hard safety rules" in the PhotoVault app's CLAUDE.md (D9).
                inside = needle in line[3:].lower()
                continue
            s = line.strip()
            # "1. **Never ...**" (template) and "**1. Never ...**" (engine), or a bullet.
            if inside and (re.match(r"^(\*\*)?\d+\.\s*\S", s) or re.match(r"^[-*]\s+\S", s)):
                out.append(s)
    return out[:6]


def safety_heading_problems(project: Path, safety_rules, confine: bool) -> list[str]:
    """A declared FILE#heading whose file exists but the `## ` heading itself
    is not in it (proposal 20, V-02, deferred from V-00's finding 7): a named
    problem, so --check fails on it instead of the card just saying "none
    found in <file>" with nothing verifiable to fix. A missing file or one
    confine already excluded is P.problems()'s to name, not named twice here."""
    out = []
    for rel, heading in P.safety_sources(safety_rules):
        if confine and not P.inside(project, rel):
            continue
        path = project / rel
        if not path.is_file():
            continue
        needle = heading.lower()
        found = any(line.startswith("## ") and needle in line[3:].lower()
                    for line in path.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines())
        if not found:
            out.append(f"{rel}: declared safety_rules heading {heading!r} not found -- "
                       f"the card shows \"none found\" until this is fixed")
    return out


# Bidi controls and invisible characters; see shown().
_REORDER_OR_HIDE = frozenset([0x061C, 0x200B, 0x200E, 0x200F, *range(0x202A, 0x202F),
                              *range(0x2060, 0x2065), *range(0x2066, 0x206A), 0xFEFF])


def shown(value) -> str:
    """A ledger or declaration value, safe to print on one line (proposal 20,
    V-02 round 2, HIGH: switches by/at, requests from/to, ids, routing keys
    and values and session all reach the card unescaped; a title of
    "safe\\nwarmup --check: ready" forges a status line, the class
    .common-rules.json was already hardened against in V-00). C0, DEL and C1
    control characters become visible escapes ("\\n", "\\x1b", ...); a lone
    surrogate -- valid inside a Python str, unprintable as UTF-8 -- becomes
    U+FFFD. None becomes "". The result never contains a real control
    character, so it is always exactly one line, however many are fed in."""
    if value is None:
        return ""
    out = []
    for ch in str(value):
        cp = ord(ch)
        if 0xD800 <= cp <= 0xDFFF:
            out.append("�")
        elif ch == "\n":
            out.append("\\n")
        elif ch == "\r":
            out.append("\\r")
        elif ch == "\t":
            out.append("\\t")
        elif cp < 0x20 or cp == 0x7F or 0x80 <= cp <= 0x9F:
            out.append(f"\\x{cp:02x}")
        elif cp in _REORDER_OR_HIDE:
            # Proposal 20, V-09: a bidi control (U+202E) reorders what a
            # terminal shows and an invisible character (U+200B) hides text.
            # Escaped as \uXXXX, neither reaches the card raw. Other format
            # characters -- ZWJ and ZWNJ (emoji, Persian, Devanagari), the soft
            # hyphen, flag tag sequences -- cannot, and stay as written.
            out.append(f"\\u{cp:04x}")
        else:
            out.append(ch)
    return "".join(out)


def _session_problem(value, source: str) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """(the clean session name, or None) · (None, the problem naming `source`).
    Validated with tools/project.py's own one-line and UTF-8 checks, imported
    rather than re-implemented -- the same rule a declared gate or read_order
    entry already follows. An invalid session is never used for a request's
    arrow direction (proposal 20, V-02 round 2)."""
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():
        return None, f"{source}: session must be a non-empty string"
    if not P._utf8(value):
        return None, f"{source}: session is not valid UTF-8"
    if not P._one_line(value):
        return None, f"{source}: session must be one line"
    return value.strip(), None


def session_from_declaration(project: Path) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """The project's own session name, when .common-rules.json names one
    (proposal 20, V-02). Read directly: the declaration's schema (tools/
    project.py) does not carry this key yet, and a project may name itself
    without adopting anything else there."""
    path = project / P.FILE
    if not path.is_file():
        return None, None
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None, None  # P.problems() already names a broken declaration
    if not isinstance(data, dict) or "session" not in data:
        return None, None
    return _session_problem(data["session"], P.FILE)


def routing_table(data: dict) -> tuple[str, list[tuple[str, str]], int] | None:
    """(label, [(key, model), ...], skipped) from the ledger's own `tiers`
    (C1-C4) or, failing that, an app's `model_routing` keyed by complexity
    words -- printed exactly as declared, through shown() at the card, never
    translated between the two (D2). "TBD" and "claude sonnet 5" are honest
    values, not malformed ones, so nothing is filtered on shape or length.
    Skipped: a `note` key (an annotation, not a route -- never counted) and
    any non-string, non-{model: str} value (counted, so the line can say how
    many, round 2 finding 3)."""
    def extract(table: dict) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], int]:
        rows, skipped = [], 0
        for key, value in table.items():
            if key == "note":
                continue
            if isinstance(value, str):
                rows.append((key, value))
            elif isinstance(value, dict) and isinstance(value.get("model"), str):
                rows.append((key, value["model"]))
            else:
                skipped += 1
        return rows, skipped

    tiers = data.get("tiers")
    if isinstance(tiers, dict):
        rows, skipped = extract({cx: tiers[cx] for cx in L.CLASSES if cx in tiers})
        if rows:
            return "tiers", rows, skipped
    routing = data.get("model_routing")
    if isinstance(routing, dict):
        rows, skipped = extract(routing)
        if rows:
            return "model_routing", rows, skipped
    return None


def page_state(ledger_path: Path) -> tuple[str, Path]:
    page = R.default_out(ledger_path)
    if not page.exists():
        return "missing", page
    m = re.search(r'<meta name="ledger-digest" content="([0-9a-f]{64})">', page.read_text(errors="replace"))
    return ("ok" if m and m.group(1) == R.digest(ledger_path) else "stale"), page


def publish_state(project: Path, ledger_path: Path, data: dict,
                  page: Path) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None, str | None]:
    """(the ledger's last recorded publish, or None) · (a named problem, or None).

    Proposal 20, V-09 (D1). Only a session's Artifact tool can publish, so
    `tracker published` records the URL and the page's digest in a sidecar,
    and this compares that digest with the page on disk. None -- and no card
    line -- when there is no sidecar (a project that never publishes) or the
    ledger switches publishing off. A sidecar that cannot be read or does not
    hold a valid record is a problem, named without echoing its contents.
    `changed` is a to-do, never a problem: --check does not fail on it. A
    missing page is already a problem of its own, so it is not also "changed".

    V-11 round 2: the third value is the chain's label for the problem --
    "malformed", "outside the project", "records a missing page", "is a
    symlink" or "is not a regular file" -- so the chain says what it is."""
    import stat
    if not L.switch_on(data, "publish"):
        return None, None, None
    sidecar = R.published_path(ledger_path)
    if not (sidecar.is_file() or sidecar.is_symlink()):
        return None, None, None
    srel, lrel = shown(sidecar.relative_to(project)), shown(ledger_path.relative_to(project))
    fix = f"republish the page and run: tracker published {lrel} --url <artifact url>"
    if not P.inside(project, str(sidecar.relative_to(project))):
        # V-11 round 2 (from V-09): a symlinked sidecar or tracker directory. Never read.
        return (None, f"{srel} resolves outside the project -- not read; remove the symlink, then {fix}",
                "outside the project")
    if sidecar.is_symlink() or sidecar.parent.is_symlink():
        # V-11 final review: a symlink inside the project is never read either.
        return (None, f"{srel} is a symlink, or its tracker directory is -- not read; remove the symlink, "
                f"then {fix}", "is a symlink")
    try:
        record = json.loads(sidecar.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError, RecursionError) as exc:
        # RecursionError: json.loads on "[" * 100000 (round 2, finding 3).
        return None, f"{srel} is malformed ({type(exc).__name__}) -- {fix}", "malformed"
    bad = R.published_problems(record)
    if bad:
        return None, f"{srel} is malformed: {'; '.join(bad)} -- {fix}", "malformed"
    if "page" in record:
        # V-11 (D1 with D9): a declared plan_page. Its generator may stamp
        # today's date (the PhotoVault app's does), so the page file's digest
        # would move daily; what the card compares is the ledger's digest.
        prel = shown(record["page"])
        fix = (f"regenerate the page with the project's plan_page, commit it, republish it and run: "
               f"tracker published {lrel} --url <artifact url> --page {prel}")
        if not P.inside(project, record["page"]):
            return (None, f"{srel} records page {prel}, which resolves outside the project -- not read; {fix}",
                    "outside the project")
        target = project / record["page"]
        try:
            mode = target.lstat().st_mode
        except OSError:
            mode = None
        regular = mode is not None and stat.S_ISREG(mode)
        current_ledger = R.digest(ledger_path)
        pub = {"stem": ledger_path.stem, "url": record["url"], "digest": record["digest"],
               "at": record["at"], "by": record["by"], "page": record["page"],
               "ledger_digest": record["ledger_digest"], "current_ledger_digest": current_ledger,
               "page_digest": R.digest(target) if regular else None,
               "changed": regular and current_ledger != record["ledger_digest"]}
        if record.get("page_unchanged") is True:
            pub["page_unchanged"] = True
        if mode is None:
            return pub, f"{srel} records page {prel}, which does not exist -- {fix}", "records a missing page"
        if stat.S_ISLNK(mode):
            # The same rule as recording: a published page is a regular file.
            return (pub, f"{srel} records page {prel}, which is a symlink -- a published page is a regular file; "
                         f"{fix}", "is a symlink")
        if not regular:
            return pub, f"{srel} records page {prel}, which is not a regular file -- {fix}", "is not a regular file"
        return pub, None, None
    current = R.digest(page) if page.is_file() else None
    pub = {"stem": ledger_path.stem, "url": record["url"], "digest": record["digest"],
           "at": record["at"], "by": record["by"], "page_digest": current,
           "changed": current is not None and current != record["digest"]}
    if "ledger_digest" in record:
        pub["ledger_digest"] = record["ledger_digest"]
    return pub, None, None


def records_a_page(sidecar: Path) -> bool:
    """True when a per-ledger sidecar is a declared plan_page's record: a real
    file whose JSON names a `page` (proposal 20, V-11).

    Proposal 22, T-03: a per-ledger record exists only for a ledger with a
    tracker of its own or for a declared plan_page, and a project's
    declaration can be read after the record was made -- so the record's own
    shape is asked too, and a plan_page record is never silenced by a
    declaration that has since changed. A symlink is not followed: a leftover
    is not read at all, here or anywhere."""
    try:
        if sidecar.is_symlink() or sidecar.parent.is_symlink() or not sidecar.is_file():
            return False
        record = json.loads(sidecar.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError, RecursionError):
        return False
    return isinstance(record, dict) and "page" in record


def project_publish_state(project: Path, page: Path,
                          ledgers: list[dict]) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None, str | None]:
    """(the project page's last recorded publish, or None) · (a named problem,
    or None) · (the chain's label for it, or None).

    Proposal 22, T-03. The sponsor asked for one tracker per project, so the
    page that is published is docs/proposals/tracker/index.html and the record
    beside it is index.published.json, written by `tracker published
    --project`. Read exactly as a per-ledger record is: never through a
    symlink, never from outside the project, and not at all while any ledger
    switches publishing off -- the project page shows every ledger, so one
    ledger's switch silences the whole page. `changed` is a to-do, never a
    problem; a page that is missing is already a problem of its own, so it is
    not also "changed"."""
    if not all(L.switch_on(d, "publish") for d in ledgers):
        return None, None, None
    sidecar = B.published_path(project)
    if not (sidecar.is_file() or sidecar.is_symlink()):
        return None, None, None
    rel = str(sidecar.relative_to(project))
    srel = shown(rel)
    fix = "republish the project page and run: tracker published --project . --url <artifact url>"
    if not P.inside(project, rel):
        return None, f"{srel} resolves outside the project -- not read; remove the symlink, then {fix}", \
               "outside the project"
    if sidecar.is_symlink() or sidecar.parent.is_symlink():
        return (None, f"{srel} is a symlink, or its tracker directory is -- not read; remove the symlink, "
                f"then {fix}", "is a symlink")
    try:
        record = json.loads(sidecar.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError, RecursionError) as exc:
        return None, f"{srel} is malformed ({type(exc).__name__}) -- {fix}", "malformed"
    bad = R.project_published_problems(record)
    if bad:
        return None, f"{srel} is malformed: {'; '.join(bad)} -- {fix}", "malformed"
    current = R.digest(page) if page.is_file() and not page.is_symlink() else None
    return {"page": str(page.relative_to(project)), "url": record["url"], "digest": record["digest"],
            "ledgers": record["ledgers"], "at": record["at"], "by": record["by"],
            "page_digest": current, "changed": current is not None and current != record["digest"]}, None, None


def publish_line(pub: dict) -> str:
    where = pub["page"] if pub.get("page") else pub["stem"]
    return f"  page changed since last publish: {shown(where)} → {shown(pub['url'])}"


def checkpoint_state(project: Path) -> tuple[str, Path | None]:
    open_ = CP.open_ledgers(project)
    latest = CP.latest_checkpoint(project)
    if not open_:
        return "not needed", latest
    if latest is None:
        return "missing", None
    m = re.search(r"<!--\s*ledger-digest:\s*([0-9a-f]{64})\s*-->", latest.read_text(errors="replace"))
    if not m:
        # Hand-written (the engine's 2026-09-13 checkpoint is): it cannot be
        # checked against the ledger at all, which is not the same as having
        # been checked and found behind. "stale" said the second.
        return "unverifiable", latest
    current = CP.digest_of([p for p, _ in open_])
    return ("ok" if m.group(1) == current else "stale"), latest


def conformance_state(project: Path) -> dict | None:
    """bin/conformance's measure(project), or None when it could not run
    (not a git repository -- gather() has already raised by the time this is
    called from a real project, but a bare/broken checkout can still fail
    inside conformance's own checks). Proposal 28, R-01: both the plain card
    and --reheat show this, and it is what `--queue` reads from."""
    try:
        return CF.measure(project)
    except CF.CouldNotRun:
        return None


def mandatory_pending_entries(project: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Every mandatory Standard change heading still pending, regardless of
    what rules_status()'s card text currently says -- `--queue` reads this
    directly rather than re-parsing the card's own lines."""
    p = RC.mandatory_pending(project)
    if p.state in ("behind", "unresolvable"):
        return [e.heading for e in p.entries]
    return []


def part_info(item: dict, today: datetime.date) -> dict:
    """Proposal 30, P-04: what the card shows for an item that has `parts` --
    its completion % and its next open part (id, title, and, when it is
    waiting, why)."""
    nxt = PT.next_part(item)
    info = {"completion": PT.completion(item), "group": PT.group(item, today), "next": None}
    if nxt is not None:
        n = {"id": nxt.get("id"), "title": nxt.get("title", "")}
        if PT.is_waiting(nxt, today):
            owner = nxt.get("owner")
            if isinstance(owner, str) and owner.startswith("session:"):
                n["waiting"] = owner[len("session:"):]
            elif nxt.get("waiting_until"):
                n["waiting"] = nxt["waiting_until"]
        info["next"] = n
    return info


def part_line_suffix(part: dict | None) -> str:
    """` 50% · next W-10.A (waiting ...)`, or "" for an item without parts."""
    if part is None:
        return ""
    suffix = f" {part['completion']}%"
    nxt = part.get("next")
    if nxt:
        suffix += f" · next {shown(nxt['id'])} {shown(nxt['title'])}"
        if nxt.get("waiting"):
            suffix += f" (waiting {shown(nxt['waiting'])})"
    return suffix


def gather(project: Path, recall: bool, corpus: list[str]) -> dict:
    top = git(project, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if top is None:
        raise SystemExit(2)
    project = Path(top)
    today = datetime.date.today()
    gitdir, common = git(project, "rev-parse", "--git-dir"), git(project, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir")
    decl, own = P.load(project), P.declared(project)
    rules, rules_problems = rules_status(project)
    state: dict = {
        "project": str(project), "name": project.name,
        "branch": git(project, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD") or "?",
        "head": git(project, "rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD") or "no commits",
        "checkout": "worktree" if gitdir and common and Path(project, gitdir).resolve() != Path(project, common).resolve()
                    else "main checkout",
        "prohibitions": prohibitions(project, decl["safety_rules"], confine="safety_rules" in own),
        "prohibitions_from": list(dict.fromkeys(rel for rel, _ in P.safety_sources(decl["safety_rules"]))),
        "goal": decl.get("goal"),
        "prose": list(decl["read_order"]),
        "problems": [], "chain": [], "ledgers": {}, "files": {},
        # Proposal 30, P-04: every open item (with or without parts) falls
        # into one of PT.GROUPS; "done" is not shown here, only the three
        # kinds of open work the card's own line names.
        "groups": {"finish now": 0, "back burner": 0, "waiting": 0},
        "test_command": test_command(project), "quick_gate": decl["gates"].get("quick"),
        "merge_gate_declared": bool(own.get("gates", {}).get("merge")),
        "rules": rules, "ruflo": ruflo_cli(), "ruflo_namespace": ruflo_namespace(project),
        "session": None,  # proposal 20, V-02: named by a ledger's or the declaration's own `session`
        # Proposal 28, R-01: conformance item 12 runs `warmup --project DIR
        # --check` as a subprocess -- computing conformance unconditionally
        # here would make that subprocess compute it again, recursing without
        # end. main() fills these in only for the plain card and --reheat,
        # never for --check itself (or --json, --since, --migrate).
        "conformance": None,
        "mandatory_pending": [],
        # Proposal 28, R-03: the shared rules checkout's own HEAD, saved in
        # every state file so the Stop hook can notice it moved (read-only --
        # this is `git rev-parse`, never a fetch or pull) and say "run /reheat".
        "rules_head": git(RULES_DIR, "rev-parse", "HEAD"),
    }
    read: list[Path] = []

    if (project / P.FILE).is_file():
        declared_problems = P.problems(project)
        state["problems"].extend(declared_problems)
        if declared_problems:
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{P.FILE} · {len(declared_problems)} problem(s)"))
        else:
            state["chain"].append(("✓", f"{P.FILE} · declares {', '.join(own) or 'nothing'}"))
        if "safety_rules" in own:
            state["problems"].extend(safety_heading_problems(project, decl["safety_rules"], confine=True))

    for rel in state["prose"]:
        p = project / rel
        if "read_order" in own and not P.inside(project, rel):
            # P.problems() has named it. Never read, never hashed.
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{rel} · outside the project (declared in {P.FILE} read_order) -- not read"))
            continue
        if not p.is_file():
            if "read_order" in own:
                # P.problems() has already named it; the chain still shows it.
                state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{rel} · missing (declared in {P.FILE} read_order)"))
            else:
                state["problems"].append(f"{rel} is missing -- derecord seeds it; fill it in")
                state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{rel} · missing"))
            continue
        read.append(p)
        state["files"][rel] = sha256(p)
        holes = sorted(set(PLACEHOLDER.findall(p.read_text(errors="replace"))))
        n = behind(project, rel)
        where = "not committed" if n is None else f"{n} commit{'s' if n != 1 else ''} behind HEAD"
        if holes:
            state["problems"].append(f"{rel} has {len(holes)} unfilled placeholder(s): {' '.join(holes)}")
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{rel} · {where} · unfilled placeholder(s) {' '.join(holes)}"))
        else:
            state["chain"].append(("✓", f"{rel} · {where}"))

    ledgers = L.find(project)
    ledger_datas: list[dict] = []
    if not ledgers:
        state["problems"].append("no ledger under docs/proposals (docs/proposals/NN-*.json) -- a plan of record is a ledger")
        state["chain"].append(("✗", "ledger · none under docs/proposals"))
    for lp in ledgers:
        rel = str(lp.relative_to(project))
        # Auto-discovered ledgers are parsed to build the compact card, but
        # they are not added to the session's prose read-order. The card is
        # the warm-up summary; a lead opens only the active ledger's full JSON
        # (or a ledger explicitly named in .common-rules.json read_order).
        # This preserves an explicit sponsor choice while avoiding a second
        # raw-ledger read of every proposal after the card was generated.
        try:
            data = L.load(lp)
        except ValueError as exc:
            state["problems"].append(str(exc))
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"ledger {rel} · unreadable"))
            continue
        bad = L.validate(data)
        if bad:
            state["problems"].extend(f"{rel}: {b}" for b in bad)
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"ledger {rel} · {len(bad)} problem(s)"))
        else:
            state["chain"].append(("✓", f"ledger {rel} · well-formed"))
        # Proposal 22, T-02: a per-ledger page only for a ledger that records
        # its own tracker; the project page is checked once, after the loop.
        own_page = L.own_tracker(data)
        ledger_datas.append(data)
        page = R.default_out(lp)
        if own_page:
            ps, _ = page_state(lp)
            prel = str(page.relative_to(project))
            if ps == "ok":
                state["chain"].append(("✓", f"page {prel} · matches the ledger (its own tracker)"))
            else:
                state["problems"].append(f"page {prel} is {ps} -- run: tracker render {rel}")
                state["chain"].append(("✗", f"page {prel} · {ps} -- run: tracker render {rel}"))
        # Proposal 22, T-03: a per-ledger sidecar is read only where a
        # per-ledger page exists -- a ledger with its own tracker, or a
        # declared plan_page, whose record names the file its generator writes.
        # A leftover sidecar of any other ledger is not read at all, so it
        # prints nothing here; conformance item 10 names it.
        published, problem, label = (None, None, None)
        if own_page or decl["plan_page"] or records_a_page(R.published_path(lp)):
            published, problem, label = publish_state(project, lp, data, page)
        if problem:
            state["problems"].append(problem)
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{shown(R.published_path(lp).relative_to(project))} · {label}"))
        unblocked = {i["id"] for i in L.unblocked(data)}
        # Proposal 30, P-04: an item with lettered parts carries its
        # completion % and next open part alongside the card's usual fields;
        # an item without `parts` carries none of this, so its card lines are
        # untouched.
        parts_by_id = {i["id"]: part_info(i, today) for i in L.items(data)
                       if "id" in i and i.get("parts") is not None}
        for i in L.items(data):
            g = PT.group(i, today)
            if g in state["groups"]:
                state["groups"][g] += 1
        switches_off = []
        for name in L.SWITCHES:
            if not L.switch_on(data, name):
                sw = (data.get("switches") or {}).get(name) or {}
                switches_off.append({"name": name, "by": sw.get("by"), "at": sw.get("at")})
        requests = [{"id": r.get("id"), "from": r.get("from"), "to": r.get("to"), "state": r.get("state")}
                    for r in L.open_requests(data)]
        state["ledgers"][rel] = {
            "proposal": data.get("proposal"), "title": data.get("title", ""),
            "status_line": L.status_line(data),
            "items": {i["id"]: {"status": i.get("status"), "title": i.get("title", ""), "tag": i.get("tag", ""),
                                "next": i["id"] in unblocked, "parts": parts_by_id.get(i["id"])}
                      for i in L.items(data) if "id" in i},
            "asks": {a["id"]: {"state": a.get("state"), "kind": a.get("kind"), "quote": a.get("quote", "")}
                     for a in (data.get("asks") or []) if "id" in a},
            # Proposal 20, V-02 (D8, D4, D5, D7, D2): each present only when
            # the ledger declares the data it needs, so a ledger with none of
            # this prints exactly as it did before this row was built.
            "readiness": L.readiness(data),
            "merged_waiting": L.merged_waiting(data),
            "yours": L.waiting_on(data, "sponsor"),
            "switches_off": switches_off,
            "requests": requests,
            "routing": routing_table(data),
            # Proposal 20, V-09 (D1): the page's last recorded publish, or
            # None with no sidecar or with switches.publish off.
            "published": published,
            # Proposal 22, T-02: the sponsor asked for this proposal's own tracker.
            "own_tracker": own_page,
            # Proposal 26, C-04: a tail-lane item whose next_check has passed --
            # read the same way tools/tracker/board.py's lane section does,
            # never a second overdue rule.
            "overdue_tail": [{"id": i["id"], "title": i.get("title", ""), "next_check": i.get("next_check")}
                             for i in L.items(data) if RS.overdue(i, today)],
        }
        if "session" in data:
            clean, problem = _session_problem(data["session"], rel)
            if problem:
                state["problems"].append(problem)
            elif state.get("session") is None:
                state["session"] = clean

    # Proposal 22, T-02: the project's one tracker page, one line. No ledger,
    # no page to check -- the chain has already said so.
    board_state, board_page = B.project_page_state(project)
    if board_state != "none":
        brel = str(board_page.relative_to(project))
        if board_state == "ok":
            state["chain"].append(("✓", f"page {brel} · matches the ledgers"))
        else:
            state["problems"].append(f"page {brel} is {board_state} -- run: tracker board --project .")
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"page {brel} · {board_state} -- run: tracker board --project ."))
        # Proposal 22, T-03: what was last published of that one page. The key
        # is present only when there is a record to carry, so a project that
        # never publishes carries exactly the state it carried before.
        pub, problem, label = project_publish_state(project, board_page, ledger_datas)
        if pub is not None or problem is not None:
            state["published"] = pub
        if problem:
            state["problems"].append(problem)
            state["chain"].append(("✗", f"{shown(B.published_path(project).relative_to(project))} · {label}"))

    if state.get("session") is None:
        clean, problem = session_from_declaration(project)
        if problem:
            state["problems"].append(problem)
        elif clean:
            state["session"] = clean

    cs, cpath = checkpoint_state(project)
    if cpath is not None:
        read.append(cpath)
        state["files"][str(cpath.relative_to(project))] = sha256(cpath)
    if cs in ("ok", "not needed"):
        state["chain"].append(("✓" if cs == "ok" else "·",
                               f"checkpoint {cpath.relative_to(project) if cpath else ''} · "
                               f"{'matches the open ledgers' if cs == 'ok' else 'not needed, no open ledger'}".replace("  ", " ")))
    elif cs == "unverifiable":
        rel_cp = cpath.relative_to(project)
        state["problems"].append(f"checkpoint {rel_cp} has no ledger digest -- written by hand, so it cannot be "
                                 f"checked against the ledger; run: tracker checkpoint --project {project}")
        state["chain"].append(("✗", f"checkpoint {rel_cp} · no ledger digest (hand-written)"))
    else:
        state["problems"].append(f"checkpoint is {cs} -- run: tracker checkpoint --project {project}")
        state["chain"].append(("✗", f"checkpoint {cpath.relative_to(project) if cpath else ''} · {cs}".replace("  ", " ")))
    state["checkpoint"] = cs
    # Proposal 21, S-09: each mandatory Standard change since the stamp.
    state["problems"].extend(rules_problems)

    workflow = RULES_DIR / "CLAUDE-workflow.md"
    if workflow.is_file():
        read.append(workflow)
    rules_file = project / "docs" / "OPERATING-RULES.md"
    state["superseded"] = re.findall(r"^### (\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2} · superseded by .+)$",
                                     rules_file.read_text(errors="replace"), re.M) if rules_file.is_file() else []
    # A declared read order may name a ledger or the checkpoint; each is read once, where it first appears.
    seen: set[Path] = set()
    read = [p for p in read if not (p.resolve() in seen or seen.add(p.resolve()))]
    state["read_order"] = [str(p.relative_to(project)) if p.is_relative_to(project) else str(p) for p in read]
    state["read_bytes"] = sum(p.stat().st_size for p in read if p.is_file())

    state["recall"] = []
    if recall:
        for rel, led in state["ledgers"].items():
            focus = [iid for iid, it in led["items"].items() if it["status"] == "in progress"] + \
                    [iid for iid, it in led["items"].items() if it["next"]]
            for iid in focus[:2]:
                words = [w for w in re.findall(r"[a-z]{4,}", led["items"][iid]["title"].lower()) if w not in STOP][:4]
                if not words:
                    continue
                cmd = [str(RULES_DIR / "bin" / "recall"), "--limit", "3"]
                for g in corpus:
                    cmd += ["--corpus", g]
                r = subprocess.run(cmd + words, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
                state["recall"].append({"item": iid, "words": words, "hits": r.stdout.strip().splitlines()[:3]})
    return state


def _joined(items: list[str], sep: str = ", ", cap: int = 6) -> str:
    if len(items) <= cap:
        return sep.join(items)
    return sep.join(items[:cap]) + f"{sep}+{len(items) - cap} more"


def _num(x) -> str:
    """Always one decimal (round 2 finding 2): 23.3, 0.0 -- never a bare 0
    that reads as though the part earned nothing was never measured."""
    return f"{float(x):.1f}"


def ledger_v2_lines(led: dict, session: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """The proposal 20, V-02 additions for one ledger: readiness, merged-
    awaiting-evidence, the sponsor's "yours" line, switches that are off,
    requests both ways, and the project's routing table -- each only when the
    ledger has the data. Kept as one function, one line-kind at a time, so
    V-09's "page changed since last publish" line is one more entry here.
    Every value that came from the ledger is routed through shown() here, at
    the last moment before it is printed -- ids, owners, by/at, from/to,
    routing keys and values -- so a forged newline or escape sequence in the
    ledger can never reach the card as a real control character (round 2,
    HIGH). This runs even when the ledger failed validate(): the card is
    printed for an invalid ledger too, so the escaping cannot be conditional
    on the ledger being well-formed."""
    lines: list[str] = []
    r = led.get("readiness")
    if r is not None:
        lines.append(f"  readiness    {r['readiness']}% (work {_num(r['work'])} · gates {_num(r['gates'])} · "
                     f"floors {_num(r['floors'])} · receipts {_num(r['receipts'])})")
    if led.get("merged_waiting"):
        lines.append(f"  merged, awaiting evidence: {_joined([shown(i) for i in led['merged_waiting']])}")
    if led.get("yours"):
        lines.append(f"  {'yours':<13}{_joined([shown(i) for i in led['yours']])}")
    for sw in led.get("switches_off") or []:
        lines.append(f"  {shown(sw['name'])} off (by {shown(sw['by'])}, {shown(sw['at'])})")
    if led.get("requests"):
        parts = []
        for r in led["requests"]:
            rid, frm, to, state = shown(r["id"]), shown(r["from"]), shown(r["to"]), shown(r["state"])
            if session and r["from"] == session:
                parts.append(f"{rid} → {to} ({state})")
            elif session and r["to"] == session:
                parts.append(f"{rid} ← {frm} ({state})")
            else:
                parts.append(f"{rid} {frm} → {to} ({state})")
        lines.append("  requests: " + _joined(parts, sep=" · "))
    if led.get("routing"):
        label, table, skipped = led["routing"]
        line = f"  {label}  " + "  ".join(f"{shown(k)}→{shown(v)}" for k, v in table)
        if skipped:
            line += f" (+{skipped} not shown)"
        lines.append(line)
    pub = led.get("published")
    if pub and pub.get("changed"):
        lines.append(publish_line(pub))
    # Proposal 26, C-04: a tail-lane item whose next_check has passed --
    # the same overdue() tools/tracker/board.py's lane section reads.
    for t in led.get("overdue_tail") or []:
        lines.append(f"  overdue      {shown(t['id'])} {shown(t['title'])} "
                     f"(next_check was {shown(t.get('next_check'))})")
    return lines


def standard_report(s: dict) -> str:
    """`standard: N of 12 hold`, then one line per item that does not hold --
    the same wording bin/conformance's own text() uses. Empty when
    conformance could not run (not a git repository -- gather() would already
    have raised by the time this can happen from a real card)."""
    c = s.get("conformance")
    if not c:
        return ""
    lines = [f"standard: {c['hold']} of {c['total']} hold"]
    for i in c["items"]:
        if i["state"] == CF.HOLDS:
            continue
        mark = "…" if i["state"] == CF.WAITING else "✗"
        lines.append(f"  {mark} {i['n']} {i['name']}: {i['why']}" + (f" -- fix: {i['fix']}" if i["fix"] else ""))
    return "\n".join(lines)


def default_state_path(project: Path) -> Path:
    """Where `--reheat` reads its baseline and saves its own, with no
    `--state` given: inside the project's own git directory (per-worktree,
    matching `checkout` above), so nothing here is ever committed."""
    gitdir = git(project, "rev-parse", "--git-dir") or ".git"
    p = Path(gitdir)
    if not p.is_absolute():
        p = project / p
    return p / "common-rules-warmup-state.json"


def do_queue(project: Path, s: dict) -> str:
    """`--queue`'s report: one line per item actually written, or a single
    line saying there was nothing new. Writes to the first ledger under
    docs/proposals (tools/tracker/ledger.find's own order) -- the "least
    invasive" target the bundle's brief asked for; a project with more than
    one ledger keeps everything else about them untouched."""
    from tools.tracker import queue as Q
    ledgers = L.find(project)
    if not ledgers:
        return "queue: no ledger under docs/proposals -- nothing to queue into"
    target = ledgers[0]
    lines: list[str] = []
    for i in (s.get("conformance") or {}).get("items", []):
        if i["state"] == CF.HOLDS:
            continue
        key = f"conformance:{i['n']}"
        title = f"standard item {i['n']} {i['name']}: {i['why']}"
        if Q.queue(target, title, key):
            lines.append(f"  queued {key}: {title}")
    for heading in s.get("mandatory_pending") or []:
        digest = hashlib.sha256(heading.encode("utf-8", "surrogatepass")).hexdigest()[:12]
        key = f"standard:{digest}"
        title = f"mandatory Standard change: {heading}"
        if Q.queue(target, title, key):
            lines.append(f"  queued {key}: {title}")
    if not lines:
        return f"queue: nothing new ({target.name})"
    return f"queue ({target.name}):\n" + "\n".join(lines)


def card(s: dict) -> str:
    out = [f"WARM · {s['name']} · {s['branch']} @ {s['head']} · {s['checkout']}"]
    goal = s.get("goal")
    if goal:
        out.append(f"Goal: {shown(goal['outcome'])}")
        out.append("  constraints: " + " · ".join(shown(x) for x in goal["constraints"]))
        out.append("  verify: " + " · ".join(shown(x) for x in goal["verification"]))
    if s.get("context"):
        out.append(f"Context: {shown(s['context'])}")
    where = ", ".join(s.get("prohibitions_from") or ["HANDOFF.md"])
    if s["prohibitions"]:
        out.append(f"Prohibitions ({where}, verbatim):")
        out += [f"  {p}" for p in s["prohibitions"]]
    else:
        out.append(f"Prohibitions: none found in {where} -- write them before any work")
    for rel, led in s["ledgers"].items():
        out.append(f"Proposal {shown(led['proposal'])} · {shown(led['title'])}: {led['status_line']}")
        for iid, it in led["items"].items():
            # Proposal 30, P-04: an item with parts appends its completion %
            # and next open part; one without them is unchanged.
            suffix = part_line_suffix(it.get("parts"))
            if it["status"] == "in progress":
                out.append(f"  in progress  {shown(iid)} {shown(it['tag'])} {shown(it['title'])}{suffix}")
            elif it["status"] == "blocked":
                out.append(f"  blocked      {shown(iid)} {shown(it['tag'])} {shown(it['title'])}{suffix}")
        for iid, it in led["items"].items():
            if it["next"]:
                suffix = part_line_suffix(it.get("parts"))
                out.append(f"  next         {shown(iid)} {shown(it['tag'])} {shown(it['title'])}{suffix}")
        for aid, a in led["asks"].items():
            if a["state"] == "open":
                out.append(f"  open ask     {shown(aid)} ({shown(a['kind'])}) “{shown(a['quote'])}”")
        out += ledger_v2_lines(led, s.get("session"))
    # Proposal 22, T-03: the project's one page, named once, after the ledgers.
    project_pub = s.get("published")
    if project_pub and project_pub.get("changed"):
        out.append(publish_line(project_pub))
    # Proposal 30, P-04: one project-level line counting every open item
    # (with or without parts) into PT.GROUPS -- "done" is not shown, this
    # line is about what is still open.
    g = s.get("groups") or {}
    if any(g.values()):
        out.append(f"open work: finish now {g.get('finish now', 0)} · "
                   f"back burner {g.get('back burner', 0)} · waiting {g.get('waiting', 0)}")
    out.append("Chain:")
    out += [f"  {mark} {text}" for mark, text in s["chain"]]
    if s.get("superseded"):
        out.append("Superseded (docs/OPERATING-RULES.md) -- if you catch yourself following one of these, stop:")
        out += [f"  {line}" for line in s["superseded"]]
    out.append("Tools:")
    if s.get("quick_gate"):
        out.append(f"  quick gate    {s['quick_gate']}")
    if s.get("merge_gate_declared"):
        out.append(f"  merge gate    {s['test_command']} -- what land runs")
    else:
        out.append(f"  test command  {s['test_command'] or 'none found -- land will land on the gate alone'}")
    # The rules value is one line, or (S-09) a count and one shown() heading
    # per line; see rules_status().
    first, *headings = str(s["rules"]).split("\n")
    out.append(f"  rules         {first}")
    out += [f"                  {h}" for h in headings]
    if s.get("rules_pull"):
        out.append(f"  {s['rules_pull']}")
    out.append(f"  ruflo         {'CLI at ' + s['ruflo'] + ' -- not run by warm-up; the loop runs per item, stop the daemon you start' if s['ruflo'] else 'CLI NOT FOUND -- Ruflo is mandatory: stop and report'}"
                f" · namespace {shown(s['ruflo_namespace'])}")
    for r in s["recall"]:
        out.append(f"Recall for {shown(r['item'])} ({' '.join(r['words'])}): {len(r['hits'])} hit(s)")
        out += [f"  {h}" for h in r["hits"]]
    report = standard_report(s)
    if report:
        out.append(report)
    out.append("Read in order: " + " → ".join(s["read_order"]))
    if s["problems"]:
        out.append(f"{len(s['problems'])} problem(s) -- fix first, or record each in the ledger as blocked with the reason")
    out.append(f"warm-up read {s['read_bytes']:,} bytes (~{s['read_bytes'] // 4:,} tokens)")
    if s.get("queue_report"):
        out.append(s["queue_report"])
    return "\n".join(out)


def check_report(s: dict) -> str:
    lines = [f"  {mark} {shown(text)}" for mark, text in s["chain"]]
    lines.append(f"warmup --check: {'ready' if not s['problems'] else str(len(s['problems'])) + ' problem(s)'}")
    lines += [f"  ✗ {shown(p)}" for p in s["problems"]]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def delta(old: dict, new: dict) -> str:
    out = []
    if old.get("head") != new.get("head"):
        out.append(f"HEAD {old.get('head')} → {new.get('head')}")
    for rel, led in new["ledgers"].items():
        before = old.get("ledgers", {}).get(rel)
        if before is None:
            out.append(f"new ledger {rel}: Proposal {led['proposal']} · {led['title']}: {led['status_line']}")
            continue
        lines = []
        if before["status_line"] != led["status_line"]:
            lines.append(f"Proposal {led['proposal']} · {led['title']}: {before['status_line']} → {led['status_line']}")
        for iid, it in led["items"].items():
            was = before["items"].get(iid)
            if was is None:
                lines.append(f"  {iid} new · {it['status']} · {it['title']}")
            elif was["status"] != it["status"]:
                lines.append(f"  {iid} {was['status']} → {it['status']} · {it['title']}")
        for iid in before["items"]:
            if iid not in led["items"]:
                lines.append(f"  {iid} removed from the ledger")
        for aid, a in led["asks"].items():
            was = before["asks"].get(aid)
            if was is None:
                lines.append(f"  {aid} new ask ({a['kind']}) “{a['quote']}” · {a['state']}")
            elif was["state"] != a["state"]:
                lines.append(f"  {aid} {was['state']} → {a['state']}")
        # V-09: named again only when it is news -- newly changed, or the page
        # (or the URL) moved again since the state being compared with.
        # V-11: for a declared page what moved is the ledger, not the page file.
        pub, old_pub = led.get("published"), before.get("published") or {}
        moved = "current_ledger_digest" if pub and pub.get("page") else "page_digest"
        if pub and pub.get("changed") and not (old_pub.get("changed") and old_pub.get("url") == pub["url"]
                                                and old_pub.get("page") == pub.get("page")
                                                and old_pub.get(moved) == pub[moved]):
            lines.append(publish_line(pub))
        out += lines
    # T-03: the project page, named again only when it is news -- newly
    # changed, or the page (or the URL) moved again since the saved state.
    pub, old_pub = new.get("published"), old.get("published") or {}
    if pub and pub.get("changed") and not (old_pub.get("changed") and old_pub.get("url") == pub["url"]
                                           and old_pub.get("page_digest") == pub["page_digest"]):
        out.append(publish_line(pub))
    for rel in new.get("prose", PROSE):
        if old.get("files", {}).get(rel) and new["files"].get(rel) and old["files"][rel] != new["files"][rel]:
            out.append(f"{rel} changed since the last warm-up -- re-read it")
    if old.get("checkpoint") != new.get("checkpoint"):
        out.append(f"checkpoint {old.get('checkpoint')} → {new.get('checkpoint')}")
    if len(old.get("problems", [])) != len(new["problems"]):
        out.append(f"problems {len(old.get('problems', []))} → {len(new['problems'])}")
    if not out:
        summary = " · ".join(f"Proposal {l['proposal']}: {l['status_line']}" for l in new["ledgers"].values())
        return f"no change since the last warm-up · {summary}".rstrip(" ·")
    return "\n".join(out)


def main(argv) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="warmup", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--project", type=Path, default=Path.cwd())
    mode = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--check", action="store_true")
    mode.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    mode.add_argument("--since", type=Path)
    mode.add_argument("--reheat", action="store_true",
                      help="delta since the last warmup/reheat, plus the standard (proposal 28, R-01)")
    mode.add_argument("--migrate", action="store_true",
                      help="move a running project onto the standard (seed, import, supersede, CLAUDE.md)")
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="with --migrate: say what would change, write nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--at", help="with --migrate: the timestamp recorded (default: now)")
    ap.add_argument("--state", type=Path)
    ap.add_argument("--no-recall", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--recall-corpus", action="append", default=[])
    ap.add_argument("--no-pull", action="store_true",
                    help="never fast-forward the shared rules checkout (proposal 28, R-05)")
    ap.add_argument("--queue", action="store_true",
                    help="with the plain card or --reheat: record pending standard/rules items as ledger items")
    ap.add_argument("--context", help="one free-text line, printed on the card and saved to the state file")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if args.migrate:
        import datetime
        from tools import migrate as M
        at = args.at or datetime.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
        code = M.migrate(args.project, at, args.dry_run)
        if code != 2 and not args.dry_run:
            print()
            print(card(gather(args.project, recall=not args.no_recall, corpus=args.recall_corpus)))
        return code

    try:
        s = gather(args.project, recall=not args.no_recall and not args.check and not args.reheat,
                  corpus=args.recall_corpus)
    except SystemExit:
        print(f"warmup: {args.project} is not inside a git repository -- could not check", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if args.context:
        s["context"] = shown(args.context)

    # R-05: only the plain card and --reheat fast-forward the shared rules
    # checkout -- --check, --json, --since and --migrate are queries, not the
    # moment to move the checkout under whoever is reading it.
    # R-01: for the same reason (and to break the recursion noted in
    # gather()) conformance and the mandatory-pending list are also computed
    # only here, never for --check.
    if not args.check and not args.json and not args.since and not args.migrate:
        s["rules_pull"] = RP.check_and_pull(RULES_DIR, no_pull=args.no_pull)
        s["conformance"] = conformance_state(Path(s["project"]))
        s["mandatory_pending"] = mandatory_pending_entries(Path(s["project"]))

    if args.check:
        print(check_report(s))
        code = 1 if s["problems"] else 0
    elif args.json:
        print(json.dumps(s, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False, default=str))
        code = 0
    elif args.since:
        try:
            old = json.loads(args.since.read_text())
        except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
            print(f"warmup: no earlier state at {args.since}; the full card follows\n")
            print(card(s))
        else:
            print(delta(old, s))
        code = 0
    elif args.reheat:
        state_path = args.state or default_state_path(Path(s["project"]))
        try:
            old = json.loads(state_path.read_text())
        except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
            print(f"warmup --reheat: no earlier state at {state_path}; the full card follows\n")
            print(card(s))
        else:
            print(delta(old, s))
            report = standard_report(s)
            if report:
                print()
                print(report)
        if args.queue:
            print()
            print(do_queue(Path(s["project"]), s))
        args.state = state_path  # always saved, so the next --reheat has a baseline
        code = 0
    else:
        if args.queue:
            s["queue_report"] = do_queue(Path(s["project"]), s)
        print(card(s))
        code = 0

    if args.state:
        args.state.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        args.state.write_text(json.dumps(s, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False, default=str))
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
